The \code{sample_data} counterpart of \code{\link[=palette_tax_pq]{palette_tax_pq()}}: assigns a colour to
each level of a sample variable, so that a modality keeps the same colour
across every figure of a document even when a subset does not contain it.
}
\section{Two kinds of variable}{
Sample variables split into two families that deserve different colours,
and \code{type = "auto"} tells them apart:
\itemize{
\item \strong{qualitative} (default for unordered factors and character columns):
distinct hues at constant lightness, drawn from the IdEst palettes.
\item \strong{sequential} (default for ordered factors and numeric columns): a single
hue with increasing lightness, which reads as a gradient. Use it for
sampling time, depth, pH -- anything where the order carries meaning and a
rainbow would destroy it.
}
}

\section{Level order}{
\code{order_by = "levels"} honours the levels of a factor, so a variable declared
as \code{factor(x, levels = c("T0", "SS", "Inoc"))} colours in that order rather
than alphabetically. This matters most for \code{type = "sequential"}, where the
order \emph{is} the message. Non-factor columns fall back to alphabetical order,
which has the advantage of being stable when a modality disappears.
}

\section{Where the palette lives}{
Exactly as in \code{\link[=palette_tax_pq]{palette_tax_pq()}}: \code{add_to_phyloseq = TRUE} writes a
\verb{<var><suffix>} column into \code{sample_data} and returns the phyloseq object,
an existing column is read back rather than recomputed, \code{suffix} lets a
second palette coexist, and \code{force} overwrites.
}

